<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="review-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Emerg Infect Dis</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">eid</journal-id><journal-title>Emerging Infectious Diseases</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1080-6040</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1080-6059</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">9204293</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2627619</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Borna disease.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hatalski</surname><given-names>C. G.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>A. J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lipkin</surname><given-names>W. I.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>University of California, Irvine 92697-4290, USA.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Apr-Jun</season><year>1997</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>129</fpage><lpage>135</lpage><abstract><p>Borna disease virus, a newly classified nonsegmented negative-strand RNA virus with international distribution, infects a broad range of warm-blooded animals from birds to primates. Infection causes movement and behavioral disturbances reminiscent of some neuropsychiatric syndromes. The virus has not been clearly linked to any human disease; however, an association between infection with the virus and selected neuropsychiatric disorders has been suggested. We reviewed recent advances in Borna disease virus research, focusing on evidence of infection in humans.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>